M4 closes the AC.OSS.2 D-1 gap surfaced by the feature-usage
audit (`.scratch/claude-output/feature-usage-audit.md` §D-1 line
366): the four-gate dispatch-with-scope wrapper authored at
amendments #52 + #74 — the harness's prime structural-enforcement
primitive per VALUE_PROPOSITION's two tests — has zero non-test
callers. The persona's actual Agent dispatches in a Claude Code
session call the native `Task` tool directly, bypassing the
wrapper. The four-gate setup chain (sentinel + manifest rows +
test stubs + plan-doc reference) never fires in production.

M4 wires the disk-side four gates into the persona's actual
Agent-dispatch path via a Claude Code native PreToolUse hook on
the `Task` tool. The hook composes alongside A2/A3/A4 in the
multi-contributor PreToolUse stanza per `merge_pre_tool_use` —
5th entry, matcher `Task`, runs sequentially after `agent_guard`
(which also matches `Task`; both share Claude Code's
deterministic-order semantics).

Critical architectural finding (recorded at plan-authoring time):
Claude Code's PreToolUse hook protocol admits only `allow` /
`deny` / `ask` decisions — it CANNOT wrap tool execution nor
run the agent on Claude's behalf. So M4's hook cannot fully
replicate `dispatch_with_scope`'s end-to-end async IPC flow
(which includes `activate_scope_with_spec` + cost-governance
reservation + agent_runner + record_dispatch_close). The dispatch
carefully scoped M4 to the **disk-side four gates** the hook CAN
apply — all four are filesystem side-effects:

  1. AC manifest registration via
     `tracker.register_source_binding(component, ac_id, source_path_glob)`
     per AC.DSA.1.
  2. Scope sentinel creation via
     `write_active_scope_sentinel(workspace_root, scope_id, plan_path, bindings)`
     per AC.DSA.3.
  3. Test stub authoring via NEW public surface
     `write_dispatcher_stub(workspace_root, spec, scope_id, plan_path)`
     per AC.DSA.2 (delegates to existing private
     `_write_stub_idempotent`).
  4. Plan-doc reference recording via the sentinel's `plan_path`
     field (already part of #2 above; the dispatch enumerates
     four gates so M4 names the field separately for traceability).

The IPC-bound chain (cost / safety / reversibility / orchestrator
gate chain inside `activate_scope_with_spec`) stays as the
explicit-call surface for in-process callers of
`dispatch_with_scope`. M4 closes the dominant production gap
(every Task dispatch with an AC declaration acquires the
disk-side gates) while leaving the IPC-side chain as an
explicit-call surface. A future amendment can extend the hook
to fire IPC-side gates if the cost-governance ledger needs to
fill from PreToolUse-time reservations; that is OUT of M4.

Hook contract (`framework/hands-off-lifecycle/hooks/dispatch_setup_hook.py`):

  - Reads PreToolUse JSON envelope from stdin.
  - Inspects `tool_input["prompt"]` for an `<AC-MANIFEST>` block
    containing CSV rows of shape `component,ac_id,source_path_glob`.
  - Stdlib `csv.reader` parses the block; comments (`#` prefix)
    and blank lines skipped; malformed rows logged + skipped.
  - When at least one well-formed row present + workspace mode
    is `dev-mode` + `LOAM_DISPATCH_BYPASS_HOOK` env-var is NOT
    set:
      1. Writes the active-scope sentinel.
      2. Registers a manifest row per declared AC.
      3. Authors a placeholder test stub per declared AC.
      4. Emits NDJSON audit line for each step.
  - Always allows the Task tool to proceed (empty stdout) per
    the A2/A3/A4 fail-soft convention. The hook is NOT a refusal
    gate — it composes alongside `agent_guard` (which IS a
    refusal gate) without conflicting on verdict.
  - Fail-soft: tracker unavailable / sentinel write failure /
    stub write failure each emit an NDJSON line and continue;
    the dispatch is not blocked.
  - NORMAL USE workspaces short-circuit to allow without
    authoring (mode-bit gate per AC.DSA.6 mirror — structural
    enforcement is dev-mode-only).

AC declaration format (`<AC-MANIFEST>` CSV block):

  <AC-MANIFEST>
  primary-persona,AC.A8.1,framework/primary-persona/src/loam/primary_persona/dispatch_wrapper.py
  hands-off-lifecycle,AC.AG.1,framework/hands-off-lifecycle/hooks/agent_guard.py
  </AC-MANIFEST>

Stdlib-parseable (csv.reader); visually scannable in a
natural-language prompt; namespace-segregated marker (verified
at plan-authoring no collision elsewhere in tree). Recommended
per plan §10 D-build.M4.2.

Passthrough on no-AC dispatches per dispatch option 3: when no
`<AC-MANIFEST>` block present (legitimate research/plan
dispatches per AC.DSA.10 backwards-compat), the hook emits a
one-line NDJSON deprecation log to dispatch-setup-hook.log
and allows the Task tool to fire. Future amendments can audit
the log to characterise the no-AC traffic shape and rule on
whether tightening to refusal is warranted; M4 preserves
backwards-compat per plan §10 D-build.M4.3.

Recursion handling (theoretical today; design-for-future per
plan §10 D-build.M4.4): `LOAM_DISPATCH_BYPASS_HOOK=1` env-var
bypass + AC.DSA.4 idempotency. Today no production caller
invokes `dispatch_with_scope`, so recursion concern is
theoretical; the env-var lands as a clean bypass surface for
any future wiring of `dispatch_with_scope`'s `agent_runner` to
invoke the Task tool internally.

